 "target": 64.0,
 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "In 1992, 28,800 bath toys fell into the middle of the Pacific.",
   "s": 0.12,
   "e": 5.466
  },
  {
   "text": "A storm hit a cargo ship sailing from Hong Kong to America.",
   "s": 5.766,
   "e": 9.092
  },
  {
   "text": "Twelve containers went overboard. One was full of plastic ducks, frogs, beavers and turtles.",
   "s": 9.392,
   "e": 14.85
  },
  {
   "text": "The boxes dissolved, and the toys popped free.",
   "s": 15.15,
   "e": 17.525
  },
  {
   "text": "So a yellow army of ducks set sail, with no captain and no map.",
   "s": 17.825,
   "e": 21.601
  },
  {
   "text": "Ten months later, ten of them washed up in Alaska, 2,000 miles away.",
   "s": 21.901,
   "e": 26.115
  },
  {
   "text": "Then oceanographer Curtis Ebbesmeyer saw a free experiment on ocean currents.",
   "s": 26.415,
   "e": 30.786
  },
  {
   "text": "So he asked beachcombers to report every duck they found.",
   "s": 31.086,
   "e": 34.021
  },
  {
   "text": "Hundreds turned up along the coast of Alaska.",
   "s": 34.321,
   "e": 36.542
  },
  {
   "text": "But that wasn't even the strangest part.",
   "s": 36.842,
   "e": 38.656
  },
  {
   "text": "Some drifted north, through the Bering Strait, into the Arctic.",
   "s": 38.956,
   "e": 41.96
  },
  {
   "text": "They froze into the ice and drifted for years, toward the Atlantic.",
   "s": 42.26,
   "e": 45.66
  },
  {
   "text": "The sun bleached the ducks white, but the frogs stayed green.",
   "s": 45.96,
   "e": 48.853
  },
  {
   "text": "By 2003, the toy company offered a reward for any found in New England.",
   "s": 49.153,
   "e": 53.854
  },
  {
   "text": "Today collectors pay hundreds for one faded duck.",
   "s": 54.154,
   "e": 56.984
  },
  {
   "text": "So how does a bath duck end up crossing the Arctic?",
   "s": 57.284,
   "e": 59.781
  }
 ],
